+++ b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-11-18-harden-apply-patch-validation/design.md
@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch` validates every operation up front in `parseOperation`, mapped over the patch with `Array.prototype.map`. `map` skips holes, `value` presence is checked with `hasOwn` only, and `op`/`path`/`from` are destructured, which also reads inherited properties. Remaining behaviour (add/remove/replace/move/copy/test semantics, index grammar, prefix rule, equality, atomicity, `__proto__`) was reviewed against RFC 6902 §4–§5 and conforms.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:** close the validation gaps listed in the proposal. **Non-Goals:** changing any operation semantics; root removal stays an error (RFC 6902 defines no result for it, see the existing spec).
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Iterate with `Array.from(patch, ...)`, which visits holes as `undefined`, so they fail the "must be an object" check (RFC 6902 §3).
+- Treat `value: undefined` as a missing value: `undefined` is not a JSON value (RFC 8259) and cannot be serialized, so accepting it would silently drop or corrupt a member.
+- Read `op`, `path` and `from` through `hasOwn`, consistent with how `value` is already read and with the library's own-members-only rule.
+- Valid, JSON-parsed patches are unaffected, so this is not breaking.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- Callers building patches in JS with `value: undefined` now get an error instead of silent acceptance; this is the intended correction.
